From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:21:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] drm/i915/dp: Dolby Vision through a converter that the
 port's DPCD finds, not only the VBT's
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The tunnel through a DisplayPort to HDMI converter (linux-9976 to 9978)
was offered only on a port that the VBT calls an HDMI one: the HDMI
socket of a board whose converter sits between the DDI port and the
socket. A converter that is not on the board was never offered: an
adapter, the HDMI socket of a USB-C hub or dock, and so every Chromebook,
whose VBT has no HDMI on its USB-C ports. Felix Oghină saw Dolby Vision
profiles 5 and 7 through a USB-C hub on an Intel N200 Chromebook with
the VBT test taken out (PR #29).

What the port has behind it is asked of the port itself, when a display
is found: the policy already took the route from the DPCD (a branch
device of the HDMI type, an HDMI sink, a converter that will not go to
FRL), and the VBT only decided which connectors get the properties at
the start of the driver. So:

- every DisplayPort port that is not eDP and not an LSPCON of the VBT,
  of display version 11 and later, gets DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB,
  DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER and DVBRIDGE_REFUSED (intel_dv_pcon_candidate()
  no longer takes the VBT's HDMI); where nothing such is behind the
  port, DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER is "none" and the kernel refuses the
  tunnel whatever user space asks, as before;
- the list of boards counts only on the port that the VBT calls HDMI
  (it is about the converter of the board: a hub on another port of a
  listed board is not that converter); a list of converters, by what
  their branch device says of itself in its DPCD (OUI, device id,
  hardware and firmware revisions, every one of them, since what a
  converter does with the InfoFrames is its firmware's), counts on any
  port. It has no entry yet;
- so a converter that is not on a list is "unlisted": Kodi offers the
  tunnel through it where the user asks for it ("Dolby Vision through a
  display converter" set to On), never by itself. A converter that does
  not carry the Dolby vendor InfoFrame leaves the TV without a picture,
  and nothing on the source can tell whether one does;
- the kernel log line of the detect says where the converter is (on the
  board, or plugged in), and what its branch device says of itself, the
  identity that an entry of the list takes; it is said again for
  another converter, not only for another verdict;
- the description of dv_pcon says what -1 does with a converter that is
  on no list (offered to user space that asks for it), which it left
  out.

The readout of the InfoFrames (linux-9978) goes with the candidate: on
the ports that it now covers it reads a DIP the same way as before
unless the vendor InfoFrame of the tunnel is in it.

Not tried on hardware. Compiled with W=1 for i915 and xe with the
configuration and toolchain of the image: no warning in the files it
changes. Host test of the policy (47 checks): the candidate without the
VBT's HDMI, the board list on its own port only, the list of converters
field by field, and the verdict of a hub on a Chromebook; with the old
header it does not build, and four mutants (the board list on any port,
the firmware not compared, a branch device without maker matching, a
port refused) are caught.

The idea of Dolby Vision through a USB-C hub on a Chromebook is from PR
#29 by Felix Oghină; the code is written again.
---
